      "dom_id": "question-119170",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The volume of a cylinder decreases at a rate proportional to its volume. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the height, $h(t),$ in this process. \nAssume that the radius of the cylinder does not change and that $k>0.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cylinder **decreases** at a rate **proportional** to its **volume**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cylinder* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate *proportional to the volume.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cylinder is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ and using the fact that the radius of the cylinder does not change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-kV \\\\ \\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-k(\\pi {r}^{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k\\pi {r}^{2}h}{\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-kh.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
